.TH GREP 1 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS User Commands"
.SH NAME
grep \- print lines matching a pattern
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B grep
[\fB\-i\fR] [\fB\-v\fR] [\fB\-n\fR] \fIPATTERN\fR [\fIFILE\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Print each line of FILE (standard input by default) that contains a match for the literal substring PATTERN.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-i
Ignore case distinctions.
.TP
.B \-v
Invert the match: print non-matching lines.
.TP
.B \-n
Prefix each printed line with its line number.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Every mitos-utils command additionally accepts \fB\-\-help\fR (print a usage line and exit) and \fB\-\-version\fR. Commands that take file/path arguments also accept \fB\-\-\fR to mark the end of options, so a file literally named e.g. \fB\-oddfile\fR can still be referenced.
.SH NOTES
PATTERN is matched as a literal substring, not a regular expression -- closer to \fBgrep \-F\fR(1). See mitos-utils-compat(7).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if any line matched, 1 if no line matched, 2 on a usage or file error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mitos\-box (1),
.BR mitos\-utils\-compat (7),
.BR mitos\-utils\-integration (7)
